MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Zeo next proposal §6.21 (ask 41). A panel row had Rename, Open Log,
Stop and Archive (or Delete); the card's row also has the eye, ↑ "Open
Thread at This Task" and ↓ "Send to Background". The rows gain all
three, with the card's icons, tooltips and conditions:

- ↑ on every row, opening the thread at the task as a click on the row
  does, for a reader who looks for the card's button;
- the eye on a task a card holds running. It is the card's own eye: the
  card watches the task (previews it, and notifies when it ends), and
  the panel shows the same preview in a pane under its list -- the
  card's preview text (0094: the served output, else the feed's tail),
  its last 12 lines, with an X that turns the eye off. One preview at a time: the eye on
  another row moves it there and turns the previous one off;
- ↓ on a running foreground task with a tool call (the card's own
  condition, now agent_ui::can_send_to_background), sent through the
  card, so an error lands on the card's row, which the panel reads back.

An ended task keeps Open Log, which already shows its saved log. The
list's rows stay one line high (uniform_list), so the preview is a pane
of its own, not part of a row.

The card gains is_watched() and preview_text() for the panel. The
panel's buttons carry screen-reader labels of their own ("Preview:",
"Open at task:", "Background:"): with the card's "Watch:" the label
contract tests counted the panel's eyes as the card's.

Tests: panel::card_buttons (an ended row has ↑ only, a running
foreground row all three; the eye watches the task in the card and
opens and closes the pane; ↓ reaches the agent with the task's tool
call). All three seen failing with the buttons left out of the row. The
panel tests' StopConnection records background requests.
